## Where things live

```
project/
  global/                    SHARED — what everyone agrees on
    state/                   the project's state, as a DIRECTORY
      core.md                thesis, open questions, settled facts, killed ideas
      entries/               one file per dated entry — never edit another's
      blocks/                one file per topic block — append, do not rewrite
    method_spec.md           the frozen spec for the current round
    data_manifest.md         what is canonical, and why
  <author>/                  ONE PER PERSON — nobody else writes here
    session.md               that person's handoff between sessions
@@AUTHOR_MEMORY@@
workspaces/
  global/params.py           SHARED — the frozen spec, as code
  <author>/                  ONE PER PERSON
@@WORKSPACE_TREE@@    dag/                     provenance diagrams (generated; do not edit)
draft/                       SHARED — the paper, figures, tables, bibliography
tools/                       the machinery, vendored — nothing to install
```

**`project/global/` is the project.** Read the state and `method_spec.md` from
there at the start of every round and write them back there — never into your own
folder. A copy in a personal folder is a fork nobody voted for.

**Every substantive change gets its own state entry** — created at the top of
that file: date, author slug, what changed. Newest first, append only, never
rewrite someone else's entry.

**`<author>/` is yours.** Write only into your own.@@INDEPENDENCE_NOTE@@

### Paths — never write an absolute one

Set at the top of each script, all derived:

```python
DATA_ROOT   = os.environ["@@DATA_ENV@@"]                    # your working data
GLOBAL_DATA = Path(DATA_ROOT).parent / "global"             # confirmed, shared, read-only
REPO_ROOT   = next(p for p in Path(__file__).resolve().parents if (p / "CLAUDE.md").exists())
WORK_ROOT   = next(p for p in Path(__file__).resolve().parents if p.parent.name == "workspaces")
```

`DATA_ROOT` mirrors `WORK_ROOT`, so a subproject's data sits at the same relative
path under the data root as its code does under the workspace.

An absolute path in a script means nobody else can run it. If `@@DATA_ENV@@` is
unset, the script raises immediately — that is deliberate, and the fix is to set
it, never to hardcode a path. The alternative to a crash is silently reading the
wrong data.

