## Provenance — what produced this, and what breaks if I change it

`tools/provenance.py` reads the dependency graph out of the code: which script
wrote each file, what it read, back to the raw inputs. Nothing is declared; it
works because every path goes through the roots above.

```bash
python3 -m tools.provenance --file <artifact>     # where did this come from
python3 -m tools.provenance --script <script>     # what must be re-run
python3 -m tools.provenance --mermaid <sub>       # draw it
python3 -m tools.provenance --orphans             # inputs nothing produces
```

**Run `--script` before changing anything upstream** and report the blast radius
with the change. Working that set out from memory is how a stale number survives
into a draft.

**This is how you run a script. Not an extra step afterwards:**

```bash
python3 tools/runlog.py <script> [args...]      # yes
python3 <script> [args...]                      # no
```

Whenever you execute anything whose output could end up reported — a table, a
figure, a number in the draft — run it through the wrapper. The wrapper runs the
script normally and appends one line to `project/<author>/logs/runs.jsonl`
(committed): script, arguments, commit, duration, status, inputs, outputs. Read
them back with `--runs`.

The wrapper is the only thing that writes that record. It installs an audit hook
that fires on every file open below pandas, pyarrow, numpy and matplotlib, so
what lands in the line is what the script *actually* opened, including paths
assembled at runtime. Called directly, the script produces the same numbers and
no record at all — and nothing will notice, because the output looks fine. A
year later the number is undefendable.

Exploratory one-offs you are running twenty times in a row do not need it; the
moment a script's output might be shown to anyone, it does.

The static graph is a strong first draft of provenance; the run records are what
actually happened.

