## Everyone works at once — there is no lock

Several people work on this project at the same time. Nothing here may be
allowed to serialise them.

The obvious design — one `state.md` holding everything — forces exactly that.
It is long prose that each round rewrites, git cannot merge two rewrites of long
prose, and the only fix on one file is to let one person write at a time. So the
state is a DIRECTORY instead:

```bash
python3 -m tools.state show                    # read it all, newest first
python3 -m tools.state show --kind entries     # just the dated entries
python3 -m tools.state new "what changed"      # start an entry, correctly named
python3 -m tools.state new --block "RULE E ADOPTED"
```

`new` creates one file under `project/global/state/entries/` (or `blocks/`)
with its front matter filled in. Two people running it at the same moment create
two DIFFERENT files, and git merges file additions with no conflict — so nobody
waits, nothing is claimed, and nothing has to be released.

`show` is a reader. It prints; it writes no file, makes no commit, pushes
nothing. There is deliberately no assembled `state.md` on disk, because a
generated shared file is exactly what two people working at once would collide
on.

Two rules keep it that way:

- **Never edit another author's entry or block.** They are append-only records.
  If one is wrong, write a new one saying so.
- **`core.md` is the one shared file** — thesis, settled facts, open questions,
  killed ideas. Rewrite it freely; it is short and changes rarely, so two people
  editing it the same day is a small readable conflict rather than two rewrites
  of a thousand lines.

### Keeping the state current — ask, do not wait to be told

There is no command for this and nothing to close. Writing the state is part of
finishing work, and it is YOUR job to raise it rather than the human's job to
remember.

**Ask "update state?" when any of these is true:**

- a result or a number changed, or a method choice was made;
- something was settled, or killed, or a direction was dropped;
- the human approved, steered, or killed something at a gate;
- you are about to commit work a collaborator would need explained.
