## /handover — checkpoint before the context runs out

When the session gets long, run `/handover`. It runs the gap check, writes the
session's entry, promotes what settled, and rewrites your `session.md` so a cold
session could resume from it alone. Then it stops; it cannot clear its own
context.

Suggest it proactively when a session has run long. It releases nothing and
blocks nobody — there is no lock.

---

## /report — the project, on one page

`/report` writes an HTML report to the repo root: executive summary, what is
settled, open issues, and a chronology. It reads only the committed records —
the state entries, each `session.md`, the run records, and git — and
assembles the timeline with:

```bash
python3 -m tools.chronology --human
python3 -m tools.chronology --since "last week" --kinds changelog,commit --human
python3 -m tools.chronology --author <slug> --human
```

That tool is also the fastest answer to a question asked in passing. "What did
Jane do last week" is `--author jane-doe --since "last week"`. "Why did we
change that" is `--kinds changelog`, because a changelog entry carries its
reason. "Did we ever explore X" is the round entries in the changelog: every
round names what it took up, including the ones that came to nothing, so the
exploration is searchable whether or not it changed anything.

None of that reads a session log, and none of it needs to — which is why the
changelog has to be written when the decision is made, not reconstructed after.

### When the changelog falls behind

You write the entries, and `/handover` only runs when somebody invokes it, so a
session that ends any other way — the context filled up, the terminal closed,
the work was done and everyone moved on — leaves the commits in git and the
reason nowhere. Nothing notices, because the commits look fine.

```bash
python3 -m tools.chronology --gap --human
```

That names the commits with no entry after them. `/handover` and `/report` both
start with it. When it shows a gap,
write the entries while the reason is still recoverable — from this
conversation, from the diff, from `session.md`. Say which ones you cannot
reconstruct instead of inventing a reason: a wrong entry in the changelog is
worse than a missing one, because it will be believed.

