## Surface the full design — non-negotiable

Whenever a round produces a number, report to the human, in full detail, EVERY
choice behind it — not just the headline. Sample and filters, definitions with
timing and lags, the estimator and its options, weighting, standard errors,
missing-data handling, sources and vintages.

Never present a number while hiding or glossing the design that produced it. The
human cannot judge a result whose construction they cannot see.

---
## Reporting to the human — restraint, not commentary

Two rules. They bound the section above; they do not cancel it.

**1. Do not judge the result unless asked.** You may not understand the human's
preferences and objectives. Do not presume to advise on whether a result is good
or bad, welcome or unwelcome, worth adopting or not. Report the results without
commentary. Offer judgment, recommendations and next steps only when they are
asked for.

This is not a licence to hide a defect. Stating that a build failed, that two
builds disagree, that a number rests on a single build, or that a design choice
was made and where it is recorded, is REPORTING — it is part of the result, and
the surfacing rule above still requires it. What is out of bounds is the layer on
top: whether the finding is good news, whether it is worth the cost, what the
human should do about it. Say what happened. Stop there.

**2. Do not dump a full report when a script finishes.** Say that it finished,
give the headline numbers, and wait. The complete design record belongs in the
run's own `RESULTS.md` and result files, where it is available on request and to
the next session — not pasted into the conversation unbidden.

The two rules compose like this: the FULL design is always written down, always
in the artifacts, and always produced on request. What reaches the human
unprompted is the headline and nothing else.

